<section class="py-16 sm:py-20 bg-slate-50 border-t border-slate-200">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">

    <!-- Article Header -->
    <div class="mb-12 text-center">
      <span class="inline-block px-3 py-1 mb-4 text-xs font-bold text-primary bg-primary-light rounded-full">
        Complete Guide · Updated 2026
      </span>
      <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold tracking-tight text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-slate-900 leading-tight">
        CGPA to Percentage: The Complete Guide
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        CGPA and percentage both measure the same academic performance, but the conversion system is not uniform across Indian universities. This guide breaks down every major university's official formula so you can convert your CGPA accurately.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The Problem -->
    <div class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
        When you fill out a job application, a higher-studies admission form, or a scholarship application, you are often asked for a percentage. And that is where the most common mistake happens: <strong class="text-slate-900 font-semibold">every university uses a different formula</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
        Using the wrong formula can create a mismatch between your application form and your degree certificate — a problem that can delay admissions, loan approvals, or job offers.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- CGPA vs Percentage -->
    <div class="mb-12">
      <h3 class="font-heading font-extrabold tracking-tight text-2xl text-slate-900 mb-4">
        What is the difference between CGPA and percentage?
      </h3>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-7 mb-5">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <div class="w-12 h-10 rounded-lg bg-primary text-white grid place-items-center font-mono font-bold text-xs shrink-0">CGPA</div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              CGPA is the average of your overall academic performance on a 10-point scale. It is calculated by multiplying each subject's grade points by its credits, adding them up, and dividing by the total credits.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-7 mb-5">
        <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
          <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-accent text-white grid place-items-center font-mono font-bold text-sm shrink-0">%</div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
              Percentage expresses the same performance on a 100-point scale. The Indian job market, government exams (SSC, UPSC, banking), and many postgraduate programs still use percentage-based eligibility criteria.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-accent rounded-r-xl p-5">
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-slate-900">⚠️ Important:</strong> CBSE's <span class="font-mono font-bold">9.5 multiplier</span> and VTU's <span class="font-mono font-bold">(CGPA − 0.75) × 10</span> produce very different results. The same 8.0 CGPA becomes <strong>76%</strong> under CBSE, <strong>72.5%</strong> under VTU's old scheme, and <strong>80%</strong> under Anna University.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- University-wise Formulas -->
    <div class="mb-12">
      <h3 class="font-heading font-extrabold tracking-tight text-2xl text-slate-900 mb-6">
        University-wise CGPA to Percentage Formulas
      </h3>

      <!-- Card 1: CBSE -->
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-7 mb-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-primary text-white grid place-items-center font-mono font-bold text-sm">1</span>
          <h4 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-lg text-slate-900">CBSE / Delhi University</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          CBSE introduced its grading system in 2010, and this formula has been the standard ever since. CBSE analyzed five years of result data and found that students scoring 91–100 marks averaged around 95%. Hence, 95 ÷ 10 = 9.5 became the multiplier.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-primary-light border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-3 mb-4">
          <code class="font-mono font-bold text-primary-dark text-base">Percentage = CGPA × 9.5</code>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-700">
          <strong class="text-slate-900">Example:</strong> CGPA 8.5 → 8.5 × 9.5 = <span class="font-mono font-bold text-primary">80.75%</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 2: VTU -->
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-7 mb-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-primary text-white grid place-items-center font-mono font-bold text-sm">2</span>
          <h4 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-lg text-slate-900">VTU (Visvesvaraya Technological University)</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          VTU's formula depends on your scheme. This inconsistency is a common source of confusion, so check your admission year carefully.
        </p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Old scheme (2015-2018)</p>
            <div class="bg-primary-light border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-4 py-2.5 mb-2">
              <code class="font-mono font-bold text-primary-dark text-sm">(CGPA − 0.75) × 10</code>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">CGPA 8.0 → <span class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900">72.5%</span></p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">New scheme (2021+)</p>
            <div class="bg-primary-light border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-4 py-2.5 mb-2">
              <code class="font-mono font-bold text-primary-dark text-sm">CGPA × 10</code>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">CGPA 8.0 → <span class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900">80%</span></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 3: Anna University -->
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-7 mb-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-primary text-white grid place-items-center font-mono font-bold text-sm">3</span>
          <h4 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-lg text-slate-900">Anna University</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Anna University uses a simple multiplier: CGPA multiplied by 10. Keep in mind that grading here is strict — an 'O' grade (10 points) usually requires 90+ marks.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-primary-light border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-5 py-3 mb-4">
          <code class="font-mono font-bold text-primary-dark text-base">Percentage = CGPA × 10</code>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-700">
          <strong class="text-slate-900">Example:</strong> CGPA 8.4 → 8.4 × 10 = <span class="font-mono font-bold text-primary">84%</span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Card 4: Mumbai University -->
      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 shadow-sm p-6 sm:p-7 mb-5">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
          <span class="w-8 h-8 rounded-lg bg-primary text-white grid place-items-center font-mono font-bold text-sm">4</span>
          <h4 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-lg text-slate-900">Mumbai University</h4>
        </div>
        <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Mumbai University uses different formulas for different faculties, which is one of the most common conversion mistakes.
        </p>
        <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 mb-2">Engineering</p>
            <div class="bg-primary-light border-l-4 border-primary rounded-r-lg px-4 py-2.5 mb-2">
              <code class="font-mono font-bold text-primary-dark text-sm">(7.1 × CGPI) + 11</code>
            </div>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">CGPI 8.0 → <span class="font-mono font-bold text-slate-900">67.8%</span></p>
          </div>
